<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PROBE E4. A COLLISION CLASS PHASE 5 INTRODUCES THAT D-FEELNAME CANNOT SEE:
  a businessKnowledgeModel whose @name (and variable/@name) equals a
  <decision>'s @name.

      decision   dup      = 5
      bkm        dup(v)   = v * 100
      decision   call_dup = dup(3)    -> 300 if the BKM wins the name
      decision   read_dup = dup + 1   -> 6   if the decision wins the name

  Section 13.7-1's measured collision table covers inputData x inputData,
  inputData x decision, and decision x decision. It does not cover this, and it
  structurally cannot: varClaimants (Lower.hs:3879-3896) is built from freeTerms
  and `decides` only, so a BKM name is not a claimant at all today. Phase 5 mints
  BKM names from the same L4 identifiers that mint decision names, so the class
  becomes reachable the moment Phase 5 lands.

  The file asks for BOTH readings at once, deliberately: call_dup needs the BKM
  to win the name, read_dup needs the decision to win it.

  MEASURED 2026-07-31, and the two engines answer DIFFERENTLY IN KIND. The
  premise that at most one reading can hold turned out to be false on one of
  them, which is why this is written down rather than predicted.

    KIE 8.44  : validator raises DUPLICATE_NAME twice (once against the decision
                node, once against the BKM node); the BUILD is clean; and at
                runtime the BKM WINS. call_dup = 300, correct. But the DECISION
                `dup` comes back NOT_EVALUATED with a null value, and read_dup,
                which requires it, is SUCCEEDED-BUT-NULL. So the colliding
                decision is not out-voted, it is DELETED, and the only warning is
                a validator message a build does not have to read.
    Camunda 8 : entirely silent, and BOTH readings hold: dup = 5, read_dup = 6,
                AND call_dup = 300. zeebe-dmn keeps invocables and variables in
                DISJOINT namespaces, so the collision is not a collision there at
                all.

  CONSEQUENCE. This is worse than section 13.7-1's "an element wins silently".
  On KIE a BKM name colliding with a decision name SILENTLY REMOVES that decision
  from the result at build time; on Camunda the same file is well defined and
  answers everything. So the emitter cannot use either engine as its backstop and
  cannot even use portability as one: the two engines DISAGREE about what the
  document means. BKM names must be uniquified against decision names by the
  exporter, and D-FEELNAME's claimant set (Lower.hs:3879-3896, built from
  freeTerms and `decides`) must grow a BKM-name claimant to see this class at all.

  Wired HarnessMustFail on the KIE leg and HarnessMustPass on the Camunda leg.
  The asymmetry is the finding, not an accident of the fixture.
-->
<definitions xmlns="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20191111/MODEL/" id="definitions_collide_bkm_decision" name="collide_bkm_decision" namespace="https://legalese.com/l4/dmn/collide_bkm_decision" exporter="hand">
  <inputData id="input_n" name="n">
    <variable id="input_n_var" name="n" typeRef="number"/>
  </inputData>
  <decision id="decision_dup" name="dup">
    <variable id="decision_dup_var" name="dup" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_dup_n">
      <requiredInput href="#input_n"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_dup_lit">
      <text>n + 5</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
  <businessKnowledgeModel id="bkm_dup" name="dup">
    <variable id="bkm_dup_var" name="dup" typeRef="number"/>
    <encapsulatedLogic id="bkm_dup_logic">
      <formalParameter id="bkm_dup_p1" name="v" typeRef="number"/>
      <literalExpression id="bkm_dup_lit">
        <text>v * 100</text>
      </literalExpression>
    </encapsulatedLogic>
  </businessKnowledgeModel>
  <decision id="decision_call_dup" name="call_dup">
    <variable id="decision_call_dup_var" name="call_dup" typeRef="number"/>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_call_dup">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#bkm_dup"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_call_dup_lit">
      <text>dup(3)</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
  <decision id="decision_read_dup" name="read_dup">
    <variable id="decision_read_dup_var" name="read_dup" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_read_dup">
      <requiredDecision href="#decision_dup"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_read_dup_lit">
      <text>dup + 1</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
</definitions>
